\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a knot in an oriented three-manifold, Dehn filling along a slope on its peripheral torus produces a closed three-manifold. Two fillings are purely cosmetic if their resulting manifolds are orientation-preservingly homeomorphic; they are chirally cosmetic if an orientation-reversing homeomorphism identifies the results. The question has four parts. Can inequivalent slopes ever be purely cosmetic? If two knots in a closed oriented three-manifold have orientation-preservingly homeomorphic complements, must an ambient homeomorphism carry one knot to the other? Does every nontrivial knot in $S^3$ admit no purely cosmetic pair? Finally, must a knot in $S^3$ other than a torus knot $T_{2g+1,2}$ admit no chirally cosmetic pair of inequivalent slopes?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ojm/1539158668}{[S2]}, \href{https://jocg.org/index.php/jocg/article/view/4778}{[S3]}, \href{https://github.com/saulsch/Cosmetic}{[S4]}.

