\section*{Kirby Problem 1.12}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
For any nontrivial smooth knot in \(S^3\), the paper claims that surgeries at different slopes cannot be orientation-preservingly homeomorphic, resolving purely cosmetic surgery. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For any smooth nontrivial knot $K\subset S^3$ and slopes $r,s\in\mathbb Q\cup\{\infty\}$, OpenAI claims
\[
S^3_r(K)\cong S^3_s(K)\text{ by an orientation-preserving homeomorphism}\ \Longrightarrow\ r=s.
\]
Slopes use the meridian and preferred longitude; $p/q$ represents the primitive class $p\mu+q\lambda$ up to simultaneous sign. The orientation of the Dehn filling extends the knot exterior's orientation. This assertion concerns purely cosmetic surgery on a single knot in $S^3$, excluding the unknot and orientation-reversing equivalences.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 306, 2026-09-23): Existing Floer results confine a possible cosmetic pair to slopes +2 and -2 with knot genus two. Integral determinant lifts produce a nonzero instanton count, while toric period and tube estimates exclude unwanted reducible and broken strata. Compatible spinor regularization then builds an oriented one-dimensional count whose allowed boundary would force a nonzero multiple of that instanton count to vanish.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 306, 2026-09-23): Theorem 1.1 proves clause (c) for every nontrivial S3 knot, but the bundle also asks cosmetic/complement questions in arbitrary closed three-manifolds and a chirally cosmetic classification, so the bundle is not wholly resolved.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 306 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

